{
  "image_name": "d21c2c6cd5112483d7e503046f4b7961.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two geometric descriptions of wave propagation through twisted or inhomogeneous media and shows how they differ for Abelian, non-Abelian, and gain/loss systems. On the left, a set of curled ribbon-like paths represents various three-dimensional trajectories or deformations. A boxed equation underneath links these shapes to two effective quantities: a scalar phase term for the scalar geometric phase (SGP) and a vector potential for the vector geometric phase (VGP), suggesting that SGP depends on refractive-index variation while VGP is tied to the path’s transverse slopes.\n\nThe right side summarizes the resulting coupling pictures. In the SGP column, Abelian cases are shown as simple reciprocal phase accumulation through a scalar phase, while non-Abelian cases add an internal rotational structure inside the mode circles. Complex systems introduce gain and loss, indicated by yellow and blue circles and wavy arrows, and the non-Abelian complex case combines both internal rotation and non-Hermitian effects.\n\nIn the VGP column, the same categories are redrawn as directional couplings between neighboring states with complex coefficients. Compared with SGP’s symmetric phase picture, VGP emphasizes asymmetric, link-like transport with left/right coupling amplitudes and phases, especially when non-Abelian structure or gain/loss is present.",
